%T A101390 46802391881021,27792556834245193
%N A101390 Number of n-vertex unlabeled mating graphs (cf. A006024) without endpoints.
%C A101390 A mating graph, or point-determining graph, is a graph in which distinct vertices have distinct open neighborhoods, that is, N(u) != N(v) for u != v. Thus a(n) counts unlabeled mating graphs with no vertex of degree 1. - _Vladeta Jovovic_, Sep 10 2026
%F A101390 From _Vladeta Jovovic_, Sep 10 2026: (Start)
%F A101390 a(n) = Sum_{lambda partition of n} F(lambda)/z_lambda, where z_lambda = Product_{j>=1} j^m_j*m_j!, m_j is the multiplicity of j in lambda, and F(lambda) is the number of unions of edge-orbits, under a permutation of cycle type lambda, that give graphs satisfying the conditions in the definition.
%F A101390 For lambda = (lambda_1,...,lambda_k), the number of edge-orbits is r(lambda) = Sum_{i=1..k} floor(lambda_i/2) + Sum_{1<=i<j<=k} gcd(lambda_i,lambda_j).
%F A101390 Hence there are 2^r(lambda) unions of edge-orbits, and F(lambda) is the number of these unions that satisfy the conditions in the definition. (End)
%Y A101390 Cf. A006024, A004108, A004110, A059167, A399728 (labeled case).
